## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about constraints in practical feature-flag settings and to design a solver using propagation and backtracking rather than pure search. It also exposes how to craft a constructive counterexample when needed.

## Key Concepts

- Constraint satisfaction with implications and disjunctions
- Unit propagation, backtracking, and a decision trail
- Real-world constraints in deployment feature flags and release gates

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal backtracker for boolean flags with given constraints
function sat(S){
  return (!S.S1 || S.S2) && (!S.S2 || S.S3) && (!S.S4 || !S.S1) && (S.S1 || S.S4) && (S.S2 || S.S4);
}
function satPartial(part){
  const v = (k)=> part.hasOwnProperty(k)? part[k] : null;
  if (v('S1')===true && v('S2')===false) return false;
  if (v('S2')===true && v('S3')===false) return false;
  if (v('S4')===true && v('S1')===true) return false;
  if (v('S1')===false && v('S4')===false) return false;
  if (v('S2')===false && v('S4')===false) return false;
  return true;
}
function solve(partial={}, idx=0){
  const vars=['S1','S2','S3','S4'];
  if (!satPartial(partial)) return null;
  if (idx===vars.length) return partial;
  const name = vars[idx];
  for (const val of [false,true]){
    const next = Object.assign({}, partial, {[name]: val});
    const res = solve(next, idx+1);
    if (res) return res;
  }
  return null;
}
console.log(solve());
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you scale this to thousands of flags with mostly Horn clauses?
- What optimizations would you add for large constraint sets?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design and reason about constraint propagation in a three-valued logic, including disjunctions of implications and backtracking in presence of Unknowns.

## Key Concepts

- Kleene three-valued logic
- Constraint propagation
- Backtracking search
- Implication as ¬A ∨ B

## Code Example

```javascript
function evalImpl(a,b){
  // Kleene: return value of a→b as ¬a ∨ b
  // assume values: 'T','F','U'
  const not = v => v==='T'?'F':v==='F'?'T':'U';
  const or = (x,y)=> x==='T'||y==='T'?'T': x==='U'||y==='U'?'U':'F';
  return or(not(a), b);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend to more complex rule sets?
- How to measure worst-case complexity in practice?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a new, well-defined operator (Maj) within Kleene-like three-valued logic and requires concrete CSP reasoning with backtracking.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene three-valued logic semantics
- a custom 3-ary Maj operator (majority with T/F bias)
- constraint propagation and backtracking in non-binary domains

## Code Example
```javascript
function not(v){ return v==='T'?'F':v==='F'?'T':'U'; }
function maj(a,b,c){
  const t=[a,b,c].filter(x=>x==='T').length;
  const f=[a,b,c].filter(x=>x==='F').length;
  if(t>=2) return 'T';
  if(f>=2) return 'F';
  return 'U';
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend Maj to 4 inputs while preserving monotonicity?
- What is the impact of introducing additional unknowns on backtracking complexity?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests reasoning with temporal and tri-valued logic, combining forward propagation with unknowns and discrete time.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene three-valued semantics
- Temporal horizons and propagation
- Backtracking with partial assignments

## Code Example
```javascript
function check(trace){
  for(let t=0;t<3;t++){
    if(trace.X1[t]==="T" && trace.X2[t+1]==="F") return false;
    if(trace.X2[t]==="F" && trace.X3[t+1]!=="T") return false;
    if(trace.X3[t]==="U" && trace.X1[t+2]!=="U") return false;
  }
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How does this scale with more time steps and variables? 
- What changes if you require at least two U's and forbid any T?

## Why This Is Asked
To gauge practical reasoning about logic constraints, Horn-SAT familiarity, and ability to reason about linear-time algorithms and incremental updates in data pipelines.

## Key Concepts
- Horn clauses
- Forward chaining
- Partial vs full assignments
- Complexity O(n+m)
- Cycle handling and inconsistency detection

## Code Example
```javascript
function solveHorn(rules, facts){
  const trueSet = new Set(facts);
  let queue = rules.filter(r => r.antecedent.every(v => trueSet.has(v)));
  while (queue.length){
    const r = queue.shift();
    if (!trueSet.has(r.consequent)){
      trueSet.add(r.consequent);
      queue = rules.filter(x => x.antecedent.every(v => trueSet.has(v)) && !trueSet.has(x.consequent));
    }
  }
  return trueSet;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to non-Horn clauses?
- How would you handle incremental updates when facts change?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests practical structuring of 3-valued reasoning via propagation on a single formula.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene 3-valued semantics: T>U>F; ¬ swaps T/F, U→U; ∨/∧ use max/min.
- Expression-level propagation and branch pruning.
- Backtracking search with partial assignments.

## Code Example
```javascript
function eval3(a,b,c){
  const not=(v)=> v==='T'? 'F' : v==='F'? 'T' : 'U';
  const or=(x,y)=> {
    const rank={'F':0,'U':1,'T':2};
    return rank[x] >= rank[y] ? x : y;
  };
  const and=(x,y)=> {
    const rank={'F':0,'U':1,'T':2};
    return rank[x] <= rank[y] ? x : y;
  };
  return and(or(a,b), or(not(c), a));
}
```

